    "['no [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching\\ndelete interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper uses `delete` to remove configurations, equivalent to Cisco's `no` command\\n2. The `switchport` functionality in Cisco is represented by `family ethernet-switching` in Juniper\\n3. Both GigabitEthernet interfaces (1/0/0 and 2/0/0) need this command to be removed to make them routed interfaces\\n4. Juniper interface naming uses `ge` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet`\", 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching\\ndelete interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper uses `delete` to remove configurations, equivalent to Cisco's `no` command\\n2. The `switchport` functionality in Cisco is represented by `family ethernet-switching` in Juniper\\n3. Both GigabitEthernet interfaces (1/0/0 and 2/0/0) need this command to be removed to make them routed interfaces\\n4. Juniper interface naming uses `ge` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet`\", 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching'}]]": 1,
